/* ============================================================
   ANSWER KEY - Database Concepts (CRAZY HARD LEVEL)
   Curriculum:   RetailMart V3
   Note: Day 14 is concept-only, so these are short reference
   answers (no queries to run/verify) matching each question.
   ============================================================ */

/* Q1.  A star schema (denormalized dimensions, one wide fact) optimizes for fast, simple analyst queries with few joins at the cost of some redundancy; a fully normalized 3NF warehouse minimizes redundancy and update risk but forces analysts through many joins - most teams pick star schemas for BI because query simplicity/speed wins for read-heavy analytics workloads. */
/* Q2.  A wide denormalized fact table in a columnar store lets scans read only the needed columns across billions of rows without join overhead, beating normalized models for large aggregate scans; it loses when the fact table needs frequent updates (redundant columns must all be kept in sync) or when dimensions are huge/rarely queried together, wasting storage and cache. */
/* Q3.  customer_tier_history(scd_id PK surrogate, customer_id FK, tier, effective_from, effective_to, is_current boolean) - a new row is inserted each time tier changes, the prior row's effective_to and is_current are updated, and queries join on customer_id plus a date range or is_current = true. */
/* Q4.  Type 1 (overwrite) when history doesn't matter and only the current value is needed; Type 2 (new row with effective dates) when you need to reconstruct "what was true when" for accurate historical reporting; Type 3 (add a "previous value" column) when you only ever need to compare the current value to just the immediately-prior one, not full history. */
/* Q5.  An EAV table loses typing, makes simple aggregations/GROUP BYs require pivoting, defeats indexing and the query planner's statistics, and makes data quality/constraints nearly impossible to enforce; a better alternative is a properly typed table with nullable columns for truly optional attributes, or JSONB only for attributes that are genuinely sparse/variable and rarely filtered on. */
/* Q6.  When storage is cheap, teams lean toward denormalizing/duplicating data to avoid expensive joins over huge tables, since the CPU/IO cost of joining billions of rows now outweighs the marginal cost of extra disk space. */
/* Q7.  Keep a normalized OLTP base as the single source of truth, then build denormalized marts (via ETL/CDC) that are refreshed on a schedule or streamed; the sync risk is that a failed, delayed, or partial refresh leaves the mart's numbers inconsistent with the live OLTP data until reconciled. */
/* Q8.  Choose JSONB when the attribute set is genuinely sparse, variable per-row, rarely used in WHERE/GROUP BY, or evolves faster than a migration process can keep up with; prefer typed columns whenever the attribute is common, needs strong typing/constraints, or is frequently filtered/aggregated on. */
/* Q9.  "One fact per grain" means every row in a fact table should represent the same level of detail (e.g. one order line); mixing grains (some rows per-order, others per-order-line) in one table makes SUM/COUNT ambiguous and double-counts or under-counts depending on which grain a query assumes. */
/* Q10. order_promotions(order_id FK, promo_id FK, discount_applied) as a bridge table records exactly how much discount each specific promo contributed to that order; revenue is then computed from order_items' net_amount (which already reflects the final discounted price), never by separately subtracting each promo's discount again on top of it. */
/* Q11. Store returns as their own fact table (sales.returns) linked to the original order/order_item, and always define net revenue as a computed expression (gross sales - returns, filtered to the same period) rather than mutating the original sale row - so both the sale and its later return remain independently reconstructable. */
/* Q12. If tier is copied onto every order row and a customer's tier later changes, historical orders would retroactively show the new tier, making "revenue by tier" reports for past periods wrong - you'd be attributing old purchases to a tier the customer didn't hold at the time. */
/* Q13. Additive measures (sum safely across any dimension, like quantity) can be blindly aggregated; semi-additive (sum across some dimensions but not others, like an inventory snapshot balance which can't be summed across dates) and non-additive (like a rate or percentage) measures need special handling - classifying them prevents nonsensical rollups. */
/* Q14. Build one authoritative products dimension (with a stable surrogate product_key) that both sales.order_items and supply_chain.shipments/inventory_snapshots reference via the same product_id, ensuring "product" means the exact same entity and attributes across every fact table that joins to it. */
/* Q15. Add a scheduled job that recomputes the denormalized table's key aggregates directly from source and diffs them against the stored values, alerting when the difference exceeds a tolerance threshold, so drift after a backfill is caught automatically rather than discovered by a confused stakeholder. */
/* Q16. Surrogate keys become mandatory when source systems' natural keys can collide across multiple source systems (two systems both using "1001" for different customers), can change, or aren't guaranteed unique/stable enough to serve as a permanent warehouse-wide join key. */
/* Q17. Defend it as a practical guideline for OLTP design - normalize by default for integrity, then only denormalize specific, measured pain points; reject it as too vague for analytics/warehouse design, where you should denormalize deliberately upfront in read layers rather than waiting for something to "hurt." */
/* Q18. A late-arriving order_item silently changes historical totals a naive pre-aggregated table already reported as final; guard against it by either recomputing affected aggregate windows on ingestion of late data, or by explicitly tracking a "last updated" watermark per period and re-running affected rollups. */
/* Q19. Idempotency means running the same upsert multiple times (due to retries or reprocessing) produces the same final result as running it once; it matters because distributed pipelines can't guarantee exactly-once delivery, so upserts must be safe to replay without creating duplicates or double-counting. */
/* Q20. Keep products.products holding only the current price, and separately keep a price_history table with effective_from/effective_to ranges; "current price" queries read products directly, while "price as of order date" queries join order_date against the history table's date range - both answers coexist without contradiction. */
/* Q21. Every added index must be updated on every write, so a denormalized table that's already write-heavy (because redundant columns must be kept in sync) gets each write multiplied further by index maintenance cost, worsening write throughput even as it speeds up reads. */
/* Q22. Normalization is a logical design choice about how data is grouped into tables to avoid redundancy; partitioning/sharding is a physical scaling choice about how (already-designed) tables are split across storage/nodes - you can normalize or denormalize a schema independently of how or whether it's later partitioned. */
/* Q23. product_categories(product_id FK, category_id FK) as an M:N junction table; the allocation problem is that if a sale is attributed to "category" for reporting, a multi-category product's revenue must be split (or duplicated) across categories, so you need an explicit allocation rule rather than a naive one-to-one join. */
/* Q24. Store the transaction currency and amount as entered, plus the exchange rate used and the converted amount in a reporting currency as a separate column - never store only the converted total, or you lose the ability to audit/recompute using a different or corrected rate later. */
/* Q25. (1) Does this change preserve referential integrity end-to-end? (2) Is any new redundancy intentional, documented, and reconciled? (3) What grain does each new/changed table represent, and is it consistent? (4) How does this affect concurrent write performance and locking? (5) Is there a migration/backfill plan that won't silently corrupt existing data? */

/* Q26. Every row version carries xmin (the transaction id that created it) and xmax (the transaction id that deleted/updated it, if any); a reader's snapshot only "sees" row versions whose xmin is committed and visible to it and whose xmax isn't, so readers ignore in-progress writer versions entirely and never block on them, while writers create new versions rather than overwriting in place. */
/* Q27. Because UPDATE/DELETE don't remove old row versions in place, they mark them obsolete and leave them as dead tuples for any snapshot that still needs them; VACUUM's job is to reclaim the space of dead tuples once no active transaction can possibly still need to see them, keeping tables/indexes from bloating - a long analyst transaction delays this cleanup for the whole system. */
/* Q28. The bloat is that dead tuples pile up in tables and indexes because VACUUM can't remove anything newer than the oldest still-needed snapshot (the xmin horizon); consequences include larger tables/indexes, slower scans, more disk usage, and eventually transaction-id wraparound risk if left unchecked for very long. */
/* Q29. Snapshot isolation gives each transaction a consistent view of the database as of its start, preventing dirty/non-repeatable reads, but it can still allow write skew (two transactions independently make writes that are individually valid against their own snapshot but jointly violate a constraint); true serializability additionally guarantees the outcome is equivalent to some serial execution order, ruling out write skew too. */
/* Q30. SSI is Postgres's implementation of SERIALIZABLE that runs on top of snapshot isolation while tracking read/write dependencies between concurrent transactions to detect dangerous structures (cycles) that would produce a non-serializable outcome - it specifically catches write-skew-style conflicts that plain snapshot isolation would silently allow. */
/* Q31. Two on-call doctors each check "is at least one other doctor on call?" (both see yes, since each sees the other), and both then independently sign off, leaving zero doctors on call - neither transaction violated the rule based on what it individually read, but the combined result is invalid; only SERIALIZABLE detects this cross-transaction dependency and aborts one of them. */
/* Q32. Under READ COMMITTED, each SELECT in a transaction gets a fresh view of currently-committed data, so if another transaction commits a change to a row between your transaction's first and second SELECT of that same row, the second read returns the newly committed (different) value - a non-repeatable read. */
/* Q33. SELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks the target row(s) so no other transaction can modify them until you commit; after acquiring the lock you re-check the value you're about to base your write on (it may have changed since your initial unlocked read), and only then perform the write, eliminating the window where a lost update could occur. */
/* Q34. Row-level locks apply to individual rows (acquired by statements like UPDATE, DELETE, SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) and allow high concurrency on unrelated rows; table-level locks apply to an entire table (acquired by DDL like ALTER TABLE, or explicit LOCK TABLE) and block far more concurrent activity, so they're used more sparingly. */
/* Q35. T1 holds a lock T2 wants and waits for a lock T3 holds; T2 holds a lock T3 wants and waits for a lock T1 holds; T3 holds a lock T1 wants and waits for T2's lock - a three-way wait cycle; Postgres's deadlock detector periodically scans the lock wait graph, finds the cycle, and aborts one of the transactions (returning a deadlock_detected error) to break it. */
/* Q36. A serialization_failure means the database itself determined the transaction cannot safely be applied given other concurrent activity, not that the application's logic was wrong - retrying the same transaction (often immediately) is expected to succeed once the conflicting transaction has cleared, so retry-on-40001 is the correct, designed-for response rather than surfacing an error to the user. */
/* Q37. Every change is appended to the WAL before the corresponding data page is modified in place (write-ahead), and a COMMIT is only acknowledged once that WAL record is durably flushed; if the server crashes, Postgres replays (redoes) the WAL entries from the last checkpoint forward, reapplying committed changes that hadn't yet been written to the data files, guaranteeing no committed work is lost. */
/* Q38. A checkpoint flushes all dirty (modified) data pages to disk and records a point after which WAL doesn't need to be replayed on the next crash recovery; frequent checkpoints mean faster crash recovery (less WAL to replay) but more I/O overhead during normal operation, while infrequent checkpoints reduce steady-state I/O but lengthen recovery time after a crash. */
/* Q39. Synchronous replication waits for a standby to confirm it has durably received (and optionally applied) a transaction before acknowledging COMMIT to the client, guaranteeing zero data loss on primary failure at the cost of added write latency; asynchronous replication acknowledges COMMIT immediately without waiting, giving lower latency but risking losing the most recent transactions if the primary fails before the standby catches up. */
/* Q40. An analyst querying an async replica right after a write on the primary might not see that write yet, because replication lag means the replica's data is a slightly-stale snapshot of the primary - the query could appear to "miss" data that was just committed elsewhere. */
/* Q41. "Read your own writes" means a client expects to immediately see the effects of its own just-completed write on its next read; it can break on a lagging async replica if the write went to the primary but the very next read hits a replica that hasn't yet received/applied that write, making the change appear to have vanished. */
/* Q42. A CHECK constraint only validates a single row's own values independently and can't compare across rows, so two concurrent inserts of the same value could both individually pass a CHECK; a UNIQUE index physically enforces a cross-row invariant via the index structure itself, so the database serializes the two inserts at the index level and rejects whichever one violates uniqueness, even under true concurrency. */
/* Q43. Advisory locks are application-defined locks identified by an arbitrary key (a number/pair you choose) that Postgres tracks but that aren't tied to any actual row or table, letting you coordinate logic that has no natural database object to lock; a legitimate use case is serializing a cron/batch job (like a nightly report build) so only one instance runs at a time, using an advisory lock as a mutex. */
/* Q44. Double-booking is when two concurrent transactions both believe a resource (a seat, a room, the last unit of stock) is available and both commit a booking for it; three ways to prevent it: (1) a UNIQUE constraint on the resource+slot combination so the second insert fails, (2) SELECT ... FOR UPDATE to lock the resource row before checking/booking, (3) running the check-and-book at SERIALIZABLE isolation so the database detects and aborts the conflicting transaction. */
/* Q45. Under READ COMMITTED, each COUNT(*) statement sees a fresh snapshot of currently-committed rows, so a concurrent insert/delete between the two counts can change the result; under REPEATABLE READ, both counts run against the same snapshot taken at the start of the transaction, so they're guaranteed to return the same number regardless of concurrent activity. */
/* Q46. An idle-in-transaction session (BEGIN with no COMMIT/ROLLBACK) holds its snapshot open indefinitely, which can hold back the xmin horizon (blocking VACUUM cleanup and causing bloat) and can also hold locks on any rows it touched, potentially blocking other transactions from proceeding for as long as the session sits idle. */
/* Q47. Splitting a large write into smaller chunks committed independently lets each chunk finish and release locks/resources faster (better throughput and concurrency), but it means a failure partway through leaves some chunks committed and others not - you lose the "all N rows succeed or none do" atomicity guarantee across the whole operation. */
/* Q48. Wrap the staging load and the final-table swap/merge in a single transaction (or use an atomic operation like renaming/swapping a table via DDL inside a transaction), so either the entire staging-to-final promotion completes together or, on any failure, the whole step rolls back leaving the final table untouched. */
/* Q49. True exactly-once delivery is essentially impossible across a network due to retries after ambiguous failures (did the message get processed before the ack was lost?); idempotent upserts approximate it by making re-delivery harmless - processing the same message/record twice with an upsert keyed on its natural identity produces the same end state as processing it once. */
/* Q50. Isolation ensures each transaction's view is correct and uncorrupted by concurrent activity; durability ensures a committed change survives crashes on that node; replication extends durability/availability across nodes but introduces its own lag/consistency tradeoffs (sync vs async) - "never wrong" requires choosing isolation strong enough to prevent anomalies, durability guarantees before acknowledging success, and a replication/read strategy that doesn't expose stale or lost data to the dashboard. */

/* Q51. CAP theorem states that during a network partition, a distributed system must choose between Consistency (every node sees the same, latest data) and Availability (every request gets a response) - you can't have both fully during the partition. */
/* Q52. Strong consistency means every read immediately reflects the latest write (checkout showing the correct, current stock count); eventual consistency means reads may temporarily show stale data that "catches up" later (a product recommendation feed that's a few minutes behind the latest browsing activity). */
/* Q53. Checkout must guarantee correctness right now (can't oversell the last item or double-charge), so it needs strong consistency; analytics dashboards summarizing yesterday's trends can tolerate being a few minutes or hours stale without any real business harm, so eventual consistency is an acceptable, cheaper tradeoff. */
/* Q54. BASE (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent) favors availability and performance over immediate consistency, fitting large-scale, read-heavy or loosely-coordinated systems; ACID favors strict correctness and immediate consistency, fitting transactional systems like checkout/payments where every operation must be exactly right. */
/* Q55. Orders and inventory living in separate databases means there's no single transaction/WAL spanning both, so a write to one can succeed while the corresponding write to the other fails, leaving them inconsistent; the saga pattern recovers this by running a sequence of local transactions with defined compensating actions that undo prior steps if a later step fails. */
/* Q56. In a saga, "place order" -> "reserve inventory" -> "charge payment" each runs as its own local transaction in its own service; if charging payment fails after inventory was reserved, a compensating transaction (release the reserved inventory) runs to undo that prior step, so the overall business operation ends up logically consistent even without a single cross-service transaction. */
/* Q57. Each service owns its own database and transaction boundary, so there's no shared lock manager or WAL spanning multiple services the way there is within one database - the network between services can fail, be slow, or be partitioned in ways a single-node transaction manager never has to contend with. */
/* Q58. Two-phase commit has a coordinator ask every participant to "prepare" (lock resources, promise they can commit) and only then tells all of them to "commit"; it's often avoided because it's blocking (a crashed coordinator or slow participant can leave everyone else's resources locked indefinitely) and doesn't scale well across many independent services. */
/* Q59. The transactional outbox pattern writes the business change and an "event to publish" into the same local database transaction (an outbox table), then a separate process reliably relays outbox rows to a message broker; it solves the "dual write" problem where writing to your database and separately publishing an event to a broker could otherwise succeed independently or fail independently, leaving them out of sync. */
/* Q60. Idempotency saves the aggregates - if the message carries (or the handler derives) a unique identifier, processing it a second time is detected and either skipped or safely re-applies to the same end state (e.g. an upsert keyed on that id) instead of double-counting the event's effect. */
/* Q61. Because different replicas can be at slightly different points in receiving replicated writes (replication lag), so which replica a query happens to land on affects what data it sees; making it deterministic means always routing a given dashboard/query to the same replica (or the primary) or reading from a system with a defined consistency guarantee (like read-your-writes routing) instead of round-robining across replicas. */
/* Q62. Pre-aggregating via materialized views trades perfect real-time correctness for speed and lower query cost, since the view is only as fresh as its last refresh; always computing from raw guarantees correctness as of query time but costs more CPU/IO on every query - the right choice depends on how fresh the answer must be versus how expensive raw computation is. */
/* Q63. When the audience (execs) makes decisions on trends and direction rather than to-the-second numbers, and the cost/complexity of guaranteeing real-time exactness (more infrastructure, more contention with OLTP) isn't justified by any actual decision that depends on second-by-second freshness. */
/* Q64. Late or out-of-order events can arrive after a windowed aggregate has already been finalized and reported, understating that window's true total; mitigations include watermarks (a rule for how late an event can be before it's considered "too late") and a grace period that keeps a window open/re-computable for some time after it would otherwise close. */
/* Q65. Duplicating data lets each service answer queries locally without depending on another service being up or reachable (better availability/latency), but now two copies of the same fact exist that can diverge if updates to one aren't (or can't be) immediately propagated to the other - you've traded a single source of truth (integrity) for independence (availability/latency). */
/* Q66. A FOREIGN KEY constraint is enforced by one database engine checking its own tables, so it can guarantee an orders row's customer_id exists in that same database's customers table, but it has no mechanism to validate a reference into a completely different service's database - cross-service "referential integrity" must instead be maintained by application logic, events, or periodic reconciliation, with no hard guarantee against orphans. */
/* Q67. Define one authoritative, versioned extraction point (a specific snapshot/watermark of the eventually-consistent source), always report from data as-of that agreed cutoff, and run a reconciliation/tie-out step comparing the extracted totals against the source once it has fully caught up, adjusting or re-running if discrepancies are found. */
/* Q68. An idempotency key is a unique token the client attaches to a "retry payment" request; the server checks whether that key was already processed and, if so, returns the original result instead of charging again - it prevents a duplicate charge anomaly when a client retries after a timeout without knowing whether the first attempt actually succeeded. */
/* Q69. If the analyst's fix write goes to the primary but their immediate re-query hits a lagging replica, they may not see their own change yet and could wrongly conclude the fix didn't work (or worse, apply the fix again) - read-after-write consistency is what would guarantee their own recent write is visible to their own next read. */
/* Q70. CDC (Change Data Capture) only guarantees that changes will eventually be delivered to the warehouse in commit order (eventual consistency with ordering preserved per source); it does not guarantee the warehouse reflects the OLTP source at any given instant - there's always some lag between a source commit and its arrival/application downstream. */
/* Q71. Wall-clock time across distributed nodes can drift, skew, or even move backward relative to other nodes (NTP adjustments, clock drift), so two events' timestamps aren't reliably comparable for ordering; logical clocks (like Lamport timestamps or vector clocks) or a monotonic sequence/commit-order id from a single authoritative source are used instead to establish a trustworthy causal order. */
/* Q72. The reconciliation pattern periodically re-runs a full, from-scratch computation of an aggregate directly from raw source data and compares/replaces the incrementally-maintained version, correcting any drift that crept in from missed events, bugs, or late-arriving data that the incremental process didn't fully account for. */
/* Q73. A single source of truth becomes impossible once multiple systems can independently and legitimately modify overlapping data (e.g. inventory adjusted by both a POS system and a warehouse system) without one being strictly upstream of the other; you choose the authoritative source by picking whichever system the business process treats as the system of record for that specific fact, and treating all others as derived/synced copies. */
/* Q74. Because the OLTP primary reflects the absolute latest state but risks adding load/contention to production; a replica reflects a recent-but-possibly-lagging copy; the warehouse reflects a batch/streamed, further-delayed and often denormalized copy - the "right" answer to a data question can differ across the three, so analysts need to know which one they're actually querying to correctly interpret freshness and any discrepancies. */
/* Q75. RetailMart's transactional core (orders, payments, inventory writes) should sit at the ACID-strong end for correctness; its analytics/reporting layer (views, materialized views, dashboards) should sit further toward eventually-consistent, refreshed on a schedule appropriate to the decisions it supports - accepting staleness there in exchange for not burdening or risking the OLTP system analysts don't need real-time access to. */

/* Q76. customers.customers (3NF - clean entity attributes), customers.addresses (3NF - one address per row, properly keyed by customer_id), sales.orders (mostly 3NF but net_total is a denormalized derived sum), sales.order_items (3NF - clean junction/fact grain of one product per order line). */
/* Q77. Canonical revenue is SUM(sales.order_items.net_amount) for orders whose order_status reflects a completed/valid sale (excluding cancelled orders), net of amounts already reflected in sales.returns.refund_amount for returned items - it's authoritative because it's computed directly from the line-item source of truth with explicit status filtering, rather than trusting a possibly-stale cached total. */
/* Q78. Add customer_tier_history(customer_id FK, tier, effective_from, effective_to) as a Type-2 table; to answer "what tier was this customer on the order date," join orders.order_date against the history row where effective_from <= order_date < effective_to (or effective_to IS NULL for the currently active row). */
/* Q79. A CHECK constraint can't compare against a derived aggregate across other rows, so it's not viable for this; a trigger (recompute/verify on every order_items change) guarantees OLTP-side real-time consistency at some write-time cost, while a scheduled reconciliation check fits the analytics context better, since a small window of drift is tolerable there and a trigger would add write overhead the OLTP checkout path shouldn't pay. */
/* Q80. Fact table: sales.order_items, grain = one row per product per order. Measures: quantity, unit_price, gross_amount, discount_amount, net_amount. Dimensions: customers.customers (via orders.cust_id), products.products (via prod_id), stores.stores (via orders.store_id), core.dim_date (via orders.order_date), finance.payment_modes (via orders.payment_mode_id). */
/* Q81. Text tier is simple and cheap for basic filters, but a tier dimension enables richer metadata (benefits, min/max points), guaranteed valid values via FK, and pairs naturally with a Type-2 SCD design to answer point-in-time tier questions - for serious reporting/integrity needs a dimension table with FK plus SCD history is the stronger design; plain text is only defensible for a quick MVP. */
/* Q82. Build a read-only view/materialized view joining orders, order_items, customers, products, and stores into one wide "one-row-per-order-line" output, refreshed on its own schedule, while leaving the normalized base tables completely untouched - the mart is purely additive/derived, so nothing about the OLTP schema's integrity is at risk. */
/* Q83. Every materialized view has its own refresh cadence, and if refreshes aren't scheduled/monitored consistently, views built on top of other views can present inconsistent snapshots relative to each other or the base tables; schedule refreshes in dependency order (base-level MVs first, dependent MVs after), and monitor with a "last refreshed at" timestamp surfaced to consumers plus alerts on refresh failures or excessive staleness. */
/* Q84. Detection: compare the materialized view's aggregated totals against a fresh recompute from orders/order_items for the affected period and alert on any mismatch beyond tolerance. Correction: trigger (or schedule) a targeted REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW for the affected date range/partition once late orders are identified, rather than waiting for the next routine refresh. */
/* Q85. sales.orders.net_total - risky if not actively reconciled (can silently drift from order_items); core.dim_region/core.dim_brand denormalized names on any report views - safe, since those dimensions change rarely and the views recompute from source on every query rather than storing a stale copy. */
/* Q86. supply_chain.inventory_snapshots' composite key (warehouse_id, product_id, snapshot_date) already captures exactly "stock of this product in this warehouse as of this date"; "stock as of any date" is reconstructable by selecting the row (or most recent prior row) matching that date, making a daily-snapshot design the natural fit for point-in-time inventory questions across multiple warehouses. */
/* Q87. An append-only refund_audit_log(audit_id PK surrogate, payment_id FK, order_id FK, amount, reason, logged_at, logged_by) table that only ever receives INSERTs, never UPDATEs/DELETEs; the durability requirement is that every insert must be part of a committed transaction with the refund action itself, so the audit trail can never be silently lost or missing an entry for a refund that actually happened. */
/* Q88. Ensure the dashboard's "total customers" query always reads from the primary (or a replica explicitly known to be caught up via a monitored lag threshold) rather than an arbitrary replica, and surface the data's as-of timestamp so any lag is transparent rather than silently presented as current. */
/* Q89. Systematically: (1) confirm both reports use the same date range/status filters and revenue definition, (2) check whether returns/refunds are handled (subtracted) consistently in both, (3) verify neither report is reading a stale denormalized/cached total (like net_total) versus a live SUM(order_items), (4) check for double-counting from a join fan-out (e.g. joining orders to multiple order_items or promotions without proper grouping), (5) confirm both pulled from the same source (primary vs a lagging replica or warehouse snapshot). */
/* Q90. A scheduled job groups sales.order_items by order_id, sums net_amount, and joins against sales.orders.net_total, flagging any order_id where the absolute difference exceeds a small tolerance (e.g. rounding cents); alert on any flagged row, and separately alert if the count of flagged rows spikes suddenly (indicating a systemic bug rather than isolated drift). */
/* Q91. Email can be changed, reused after account deletion, or duplicated by user error, so joining on it risks silently merging two different real customers or missing matches after an email change; customer_id is stable and guaranteed unique for the life of the record, making it the only safe cross-system join key, with email treated as a mutable attribute, not an identifier. */
/* Q92. Without a time component, all orders on the same calendar day collapse to midnight, making "orders per hour" impossible to compute accurately from order_date alone; the correct alternative source is a finer-grained timestamp, such as when the order or its payment was actually recorded (e.g. sales.payments.payment_date if it carries time, or an application-level created_at timestamp), assuming one is captured upstream. */
/* Q93. Designate products.products as the single conformed product dimension (with product_id as the shared key), and require sales.order_items, supply_chain.shipments/inventory_snapshots, and manufacture.work_orders to all reference that same product_id rather than maintaining separate product lists, so "product" means one consistent entity across sales, supply chain, and manufacturing reporting. */
/* Q94. Add a store_region_history(store_id FK, region_id FK, effective_from, effective_to) Type-2 table (mirroring the tier-history pattern), and compute revenue-by-region by joining each order's order_date against the store's region as of that date from the history table, rather than the store's current region_id. */
/* Q95. The key challenge is that web_events.page_views.customer_id may be NULL for anonymous browsing (only session_id is reliably present), and matching a session to a later order requires bridging session_id to customer_id (once they log in or checkout) plus reasoning about attribution windows/timing (how long after a page view does an order still "count" as attributed to it) - both identity-resolution and time-window consistency are real challenges. */
/* Q96. Deleting a customer would either cascade-delete their entire order/payment/return history (destroying financial/audit records regulators or the business need) or fail/orphan data if restricted; the compliant design is to anonymize instead - null out or hash PII columns (name, email, phone) on customers.customers while keeping the customer_id row and all its foreign-key relationships intact, satisfying deletion requests without breaking referential integrity or historical reporting. */
/* Q97. Long analyst queries on the primary hold back VACUUM (per Q27/Q28's bloat/xmin-horizon issue) and compete for locks/resources with live OLTP writes, risking both production performance and correctness for concurrent transactions; isolating analysts onto a replica or warehouse removes that contention entirely, letting OLTP maintain its needed throughput and low-latency writes undisturbed. */
/* Q98. Real-time and always-correct and cheap can't all be maximized simultaneously per CAP/consistency tradeoffs: real-time-and-correct requires querying a strongly consistent source with minimal staleness, which is expensive (contends with OLTP or requires costly synchronous infrastructure); making it cheap typically means batching/caching (denormalized marts, async replicas), which reintroduces staleness - the stakeholder must accept staleness, cost, or scope tradeoffs, not demand all three unconditionally. */
/* Q99. (1) FK-orphan check: order_items.prod_id / order_id referencing missing rows. (2) Total-drift check: orders.net_total vs SUM(order_items.net_amount). (3) Duplicate-key check: unexpected duplicate values in supposedly-unique business keys (e.g. duplicate emails). (4) NULL-key check: required FK columns (cust_id, store_id) that are unexpectedly NULL. (5) Status-sanity check: order_status/payment status/shipment status values outside the expected enumerated set, or logically inconsistent combinations (e.g. a "delivered" shipment with no shipped_date). */
/* Q100. RetailMart's design philosophy treats its OLTP core as the non-negotiable source of truth - fully normalized, constraint-enforced, and transactionally safe for every customer-facing write - while pushing every performance or convenience tradeoff (denormalized totals, dimension flattening, wide reporting marts) into clearly-labeled derived layers (views, materialized views) that are always reconcilable back to source; it accepts controlled, monitored staleness in analytics in exchange for never risking the correctness of the transactional data itself, and it deliberately isolates analyst workloads from OLTP contention so integrity and usability can both be optimized without compromising each other. */

